Developer Project Handoff
Project Objective

Build a centralized commission management system for TruePlan FL Inc., a Medicare and insurance brokerage operating with Career Agents and Independent Agents.

The system needs to import insurance carrier commission statements, identify production ownership, calculate compensation, track agency revenue, and generate agent commission statements.

The long-term goal is a secure, web-based application integrated with GoHighLevel (GHL).
1. Work Already Completed

A Node.js prototype has been developed using SQLite.

Current functionality includes:

    Extracting commission transactions from an Ultimate Health Plans PDF statement.

    Converting extracted transactions into structured JSON.

    Validating the extracted commission total against the statement.

    Importing transactions into a SQLite database.

    Preventing duplicate imports of the same statement.

    Creating Career Agent records with compensation levels.

    Storing initial life insurance commission percentages.

    Separating personal production from agency production for management reporting.

Successful test:

September 2026 Ultimate Health Plans statement:

    Five renewal transactions imported.

    Total commission revenue: $144.60.

    Reimport test: zero new transactions and five duplicates skipped.

    All five transactions classified as Ryan Ball — Personal Production.

    No Career Agent payouts created.

This is an early prototype, not a production-ready commission system. The parser currently supports the tested Ultimate statement format; other carriers and statement layouts still require development.
2. Revenue and Production Structure

There are two management-reporting categories:

TruePlan FL Inc. — Agency Production

Tracks commissions associated with agency operations, Career Agent production, agency overrides, and applicable agency revenue.

Ryan Ball — Personal Production

Tracks policies personally written by the owner and their related commissions.

Both categories may receive deposits into the same bank account. They must remain separate for internal financial reporting.

These categories do not necessarily represent separate legal or tax entities. Preserve the actual carrier payee and legal entity information.
3. Agent Types

Career Agents

    Operate under TruePlan's Career Agent model.

    Have assigned compensation levels.

    TruePlan calculates and pays their commissions.

    Commissions are paid biweekly.

    Agent payouts must be based on eligible carrier commissions actually received.

    All payouts require owner review and approval before payment.

Independent Agents

    Generally receive their commissions directly from insurance carriers.

    Need access to their own production and commission reporting.

    TruePlan must track applicable agency overrides separately.

    Each independent agent or agency must only see authorized information.

4. Career Agent Levels

The system must support these four levels:

    Benefit Consultant

    Senior Benefit Consultant

    Client Advisor

    Private Client Advisor

Initial life insurance commission percentages entered into the prototype:
Level	Life Initial Commission
Benefit Consultant	70%
Senior Benefit Consultant	75%
Client Advisor	80%
Private Client Advisor	85%

These rates are preliminary configuration and should remain editable, with effective dates and historical preservation.

The system must also support Medicare Advantage, Medicare Supplement, ACA, annuities, hospital indemnity, cancer, and other insurance products.

Medicare Advantage compensation discussed previously includes tentative Career Agent rates of $300 for T65, $100 for plan changes, and $7 for applicable renewals. These should be confirmed before configuration.

Other compensation schedules are not finalized. Missing rates must remain unconfigured, not automatically treated as zero.
5. Commission Processing

Required workflow:

    Import a carrier statement.

    Parse and normalize each transaction.

    Validate statement totals.

    Detect duplicate transactions.

    Match transactions to members, policies, writing agents, and production entities.

    Identify commission types: initial, renewal, adjustment, chargeback, override, etc.

    Match commission records to actual carrier payment receipts.

    Apply the correct compensation rules.

    Calculate agent earnings and agency margins.

    Generate biweekly payout batches.

    Present payouts for owner approval.

    Record approved and completed payments.

    Maintain a complete audit trail.

The system must handle corrections, reversals, negative commissions, chargebacks, and carrier statement reprocessing.

It should never automatically pay agents simply because a commission appears on a statement.
6. Commission Calculation Requirements

The calculation engine must support:

    Fixed-dollar compensation.

    Percentage-based compensation.

    Percentage of first-year annual premium.

    Renewal compensation.

    Carrier-specific compensation.

    Product-specific compensation.

    Agent-level compensation.

    Effective-dated rate schedules.

    Overrides and hierarchy-based compensation.

    Chargebacks and adjustments.

Agent compensation and actual carrier revenue must be stored separately.
7. Dashboard Requirements

Administrator Dashboard

    Total carrier commissions.

    TruePlan agency production.

    Owner personal production.

    Career Agent compensation obligations.

    Independent Agent production and overrides.

    Unassigned transactions.

    Commission discrepancies.

    Carrier statement import history.

    Pending payout approvals.

    Paid versus unpaid commissions.

    Reporting by carrier, product, agent, agency, and period.

Career Agent Dashboard

    Individual production.

    Earned commissions.

    Pending commissions.

    Approved payouts.

    Payment history.

    Chargebacks and adjustments.

Independent Agent Dashboard

    Individual or agency production.

    Carrier commission information where available.

    Applicable overrides and reporting.

    No visibility into unrelated agents or agencies.

8. Technology Direction

The current prototype uses:

    Node.js

    JavaScript

    SQLite

    better-sqlite3

    pdf-parse

We are considering Budibase, Retool, or Appsmith for the dashboard rather than developing the entire frontend from scratch.

The developer should evaluate the best option based on security, permission controls, maintainability, cost, and scalability.

For production, evaluate a hosted relational database such as PostgreSQL, secure authentication, role-based permissions, tenant isolation, backups, and audit logging.
9. GHL Integration

TruePlan uses GoHighLevel as its CRM.

Future integration should support:

    Matching agents and customers.

    Connecting policy production to CRM contacts.

    Updating commission-related information.

    Supporting agency subaccounts.

    Synchronizing appropriate production and payment statuses.

The commission database should remain the authoritative source for financial calculations. CRM data should not independently determine payable commissions.
10. Developer Priorities

Phase 1 — Foundation

    Review the existing code and database.

    Validate statement extraction and transaction accuracy.

    Improve duplicate detection.

    Establish secure production architecture.

    Build a usable administrator dashboard.

Phase 2 — Commission Engine

    Agent and policy matching.

    Configurable commission schedules.

    Carrier payment reconciliation.

    Chargebacks and overrides.

    Biweekly payout calculation and approval.

Phase 3 — Agent Portals

    Secure Career Agent access.

    Secure Independent Agent access.

    Statements and payment histories.

    Agent-level data restrictions.

Phase 4 — Integrations and Scale

    GHL integration.

    Additional carrier statement formats.

    Automated statement ingestion where authorized.

    Reporting, exports, and operational monitoring.

11. Important Requirements

    Never mix personal production with agency production in management reports.

    Never generate agent payouts without verifying eligibility and carrier receipt.

    Never overwrite historical commission rates when schedules change.

    Never assume an unidentified transaction belongs to an agent.

    Never expose one agent's private records to another.

    Never store carrier portal passwords in application source code.

    Maintain an audit trail for imports, assignments, calculations, approvals, and adjustments.

Expected Deliverable

A secure, maintainable commission management application that allows TruePlan to process multiple carriers, manage different agent compensation structures, approve biweekly payouts, and provide accurate financial reporting through administrator and agent dashboards.